.TH LONGWIRE 1 "November 2026" "longwire 0.5.54" "longwire manual"
.SH NAME
longwire \- one long wire between your sites: a layer-2 VPN you operate by dialing a number
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B longwire
.RI [ command " [" argument " ...]]"
.br
.B longwire on
.RI [ profile ]
.br
.B longwire dial
.I number
.br
.B longwire up
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B longwire
stretches one Ethernet segment across the internet.
You run it on each machine or at each site, dial a number, and the LANs become one subnet:
ARP, DHCP, mDNS and everything else on a LAN work across the distance.
Every node is equal; a client is also a server.
A small rendezvous server, the
.BR longwire-switchboard (1),
gives each node its number and introduces two nodes that want to talk.
It then steps aside, and the traffic goes directly between them, end-to-end encrypted.
.PP
The node never runs as root.
Bridging a whole site needs one capability, granted once by the service manager;
joining a single Linux host needs one tap device it owns, which
.B longwire jack
creates, asking for a password when it has to be made.
.PP
.B longwire
is a script for the hibr shell (the engine is the hibr module
.IR longwire.so ).
It has a graphical side as well: the Control Panel pane
.B Longwire
and a telephone on the menu bar do everything described here.
See
.IR /usr/share/doc/longwire/DESKTOP.md .
.PP
With no command,
.B longwire
turns the node on from the default profile if it is off, then says how it is.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Turning it on and off
.TP
.BR on " [" \fIprofile\fR ]
Start the node in the background from a saved profile (the default one if none is named),
make the jack if it is missing, and say the number the switchboard gave.
Saying it again reports that it is already on.
.TP
.B off
Stop the node.
When the profile it was turned on with says
.BR "keep_jack = no" ,
take the jack away too (one more password, unless polkit's org.longwire.jack is set to ask none).
Same as
.BR down .
.TP
.BR up " [" \fIoptions\fR ]
Run the node in the foreground, printing what happens as sentences.
This is what a service unit runs.
With no switchboard named it uses the default profile;
.B up \-\-profile
.I name
uses a named one; anything on the command line overrides the profile.
See
.BR OPTIONS .
.TP
.B show
How the node is, in a few lines and no JSON:
your number, the switchboard, who is connected and what they have carried with each call's safety phrase, the line, and any ringing call.
With everything through the line and no gateway, its
.B way out
line names the member it goes out through, or warns that none shares its internet now, so nothing goes out;
on a machine that shares, a
.B sharing
line says so.
When the switchboard has not answered for 15 seconds, a line says so and why, as far as the node can tell.
.TP
.B status
What the node is doing, as JSON.
.TP
.B doctor
Say whether the scripts (from the hibr catalogue), the installed engine (from the package) and the running node agree on the version, and what to do if not.
A running node keeps the engine it started with, so after an upgrade it must be turned off and on.
.TP
.B watch
The node's events as sentences, as they happen.
It only listens: Ctrl-C leaves and the node goes on.
.TP
.B down
Stop the node.
.SS Calls
.TP
.BI dial " number"
Call a number such as
.BR 447-120-933 ,
a full number such as
.BR "'+0731042 447-120-933'" ,
(quoted: it is one argument), or a full address
.RB ( lw1... ).
A full address is looked up in the switchboard's directory, so it works only when the switchboard has one and the node is listed in it.
A name cannot be dialled: find its number with
.BI who " name" .
.TP
.BR pickup " [" \fIid\fR ]
Answer the ringing call.
Picking up adds the caller to your network (your line): it stays a member, and reconnects by itself, until you evict it.
.I id
is the call id from the ring, needed only when several rings are waiting.
.TP
.BR reject " [" \fIid\fR ]
Decline the ringing call.
.TP
.BR hangup " [" \fInumber\fR ]
With no number: end every call and leave the line.
With a number: end just that call.
.TP
.BR answer " [" ask | known | auto | never ]
Show, or change, how the running node answers rings.
It is kept in
.I ~/.longwire/answer
of the user who runs it, so it changes only a node that user started; for a service, change
.B \-\-answer
in
.I /etc/longwire/NIC.conf
and restart it.
See
.BR "ANSWER POLICY" .
.TP
.BI who " number"
What this node knows of a number: its key id and full address, whether it is pinned, on your line, connected (and by what path), and the safety phrase of a live call.
.TP
.BI who " name"
Ask the switchboard's directory for listed nodes whose name contains the text, or that hold a full address
.RB ( lw1... ).
.TP
.BI unpin " number"
Forget a number's key.
The next call from it is a first call again.
.TP
.BR verify " [" \fInumber\fR " " \fIwords\fR ...]
With nothing: each call, its safety phrase, and whether it is verified.
With a number and the words the other end shows: compare them with this end's (any case, any spacing).
A match is recorded in
.I ~/.longwire/verified
and marked in
.BR show ;
a difference ends the call and forgets the number's key.
.SS The line
.TP
.B peers
The line you are on: its members and which of them are connected (JSON).
.TP
.BI evict " number"
Remove a member from the line for good and change the line key.
On a line with an operator only the operator evicts: use
.BR revoke .
.TP
.BI revoke " eviction"
Carry the operator's signed eviction (hex, or a file holding it) to the line.
.TP
.BI "operator new" " file"
Make an operator's key; the file is its seed.
Keep it offline.
.TP
.BI "operator authority" " file"
Show an operator key's public key.
.TP
.BI "operator admit" " file lineid node " [ days ]
Sign an admit for a node (its
.B lw1...
address or key id) on the line
.IR lineid ,
good for
.I days
(1 to 400, default 90). An operator's admit has an end; the node is warned 14, 3 and 1 day before, and is off the line when it comes.
.TP
.BI "operator evict" " file lineid node"
Sign an eviction.
.TP
.BI "operator roll" " file lineid"
Sign a roll: no admit made before now is good any more, and every eviction older than it can be forgotten. For a line that holds as many evictions as it can, or when every key so far is suspect.
.TP
.BI roll " roll"
Carry the operator's signed roll (hex, or a file) to the line.
.TP
.BI renew " admit"
Give the running node a renewed admit (hex, or a file) from its operator, before the old one ends or after.
.SS Saved connections
.TP
.B profile list
The profiles; the default one is marked with an asterisk.
.TP
.BR "profile show" " [" \fIname\fR ]
A profile's fields.
The token is shown only as its length.
.TP
.BI "profile set" " name key" = value " ..."
Create or change a profile.
The keys are
.BR switchboard ,
.BR token ,
.BR attach " (" jack " or " splice ),
.BR interface ,
.BR address " (" \fIA.B.C.D/N\fR " or " dhcp ),
.BR gateway ,
.BR routes ,
.BR everything " (" yes " or " no ),
.BR dns ,
.BR keep_jack " (" yes " or " no ),
.BR name ,
.BR listed " (" yes " or " no ),
.BR answer " (" ask ", " known ", " auto " or " never ),
.BR exit " (" yes " or " no ),
and
.B exit_through
(a member's number);
see
.BR longwire-profiles (5).
An empty value removes the field.
The first profile you make is the default.
.TP
.BI "profile token" " name"
Set the token from standard input, so that it is never on a command line.
.TP
.BI "profile default" " name"
Make a profile the default.
.TP
.BI "profile rename" " old new"
Rename a profile. Its settings, its token and its place in the list stay, and so does being the default.
.TP
.BI "profile remove" " name"
Delete a profile.
.TP
.BR jack " " \-\-remove " [" \fItap\fR ]
Take the jack away, with a DHCP client of ours on it; only one that is the user's own.
.TP
.BR jack " [" \-\-address " " \fIA.B.C.D/N\fR | dhcp "] [" \-\-via " " \fIgateway\fR " [" \-\-routes " " \fInet/prefix,...\fR "]] [" \-\-everything "] [" \-\-dns " " \fInameserver,...\fR "] | " \-\-share "] [" \fItap\fR ]
Make the tap device a single machine attaches to
.RB ( lw0
to
.BR lw99 ,
.B lw0
by default) and give it to you, with an IPv4 address if one is given.
A small root helper does it through polkit, which asks for a password.
The device, its address, a DHCP client and routes are polkit's org.longwire.jack
.RI ( /usr/lib/longwire/jack );
with
.B \-\-everything
or
.B \-\-share
the same helper is run as
.I /usr/lib/longwire/jack\-everything
(org.longwire.everything), a permission of its own, so allowing the first with no password in Control Panel > Administrative Access does not allow rerouting the machine.
The device does not survive a restart, so it is asked for again then.
.B on
does this by itself when the jack, or its address, is missing.
.B \-\-everything
with
.B \-\-via
sends all of this machine's traffic to that gateway;
without it, out through the member of the line that shares its internet, and then it needs only the jack's own address, fixed or
.BR dhcp .
.B \-\-share
shares this machine's internet with the line: forwarding on for the jack and for the device the machine's own default route leaves by, and an nftables table of the jack's own
.RB ( longwire\-lwN )
that lets the line's network out through that device, masqueraded, lets only the answers back in, and lets nothing from the line reach the machine's other networks.
It needs a fixed address and
.BR nft ,
does not go with
.BR \-\-everything ,
and is undone exactly when the jack is made again without it, or removed.
.TP
.BR exit " [" on | off "] [" \fIprofile\fR ]
Share this machine's internet with the line, or stop:
set the profile's
.B exit
key (the running profile's, else the default one) and, if the node is on with that profile, turn it off and on again so that the jack helper sets the sharing up (one password, for org.longwire.everything).
With neither, say whether the profile shares.
It needs a jack with a fixed address and no
.BR everything ,
and nftables on the machine.
The other members use it with
.B everything = yes
and no
.B gateway
in their profiles: no address of a gateway is typed anywhere.
The exit sees everything its users send outside the line, as their internet provider would, and the internet sees its address; see
.BR longwire-security (7).
.TP
.B site \fInic\fR [\fIprofile\fR]
.TQ
.B site \-\-stop \fInic\fR
Make this machine a site node for the network card
.IR nic :
a service,
.BR longwire@ \fInic\fR,
started by a root helper after one password, with the profile's switchboard, token, name, answer policy and
.BR listed .
.B \-\-stop
stops and disables it.
.TP
.B setup splice \fInic\fR
.TQ
.B setup jack \fR[\fItap\fR [\fIaddr\fR]]
Print the one-time privileged step for an attach mode, to run by hand.
.SS Identity
.TP
.B id
Your key id (hex).
.TP
.B address
Your full address
.RB ( lw1... ).
.TP
.B bundle
Your public bundle (hex), for
.BR \-\-peer .
.TP
.B key export
Print the identity as 33 words (32 for the seed and one of checksum).
It prints to the terminal only.
.TP
.B key import
Read the 33 words on standard input, check the checksum, and restore the identity.
.SH OPTIONS
These are the options of
.BR up .
The engine takes the ones not listed as handled by the script.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " name"
Use a saved profile.
.TP
.BI \-\-switchboard " HOST:PORT/KEY"
The switchboard to register with.
.I KEY
is its full address
.RB ( lw1... )
or 64 hex digits.
.TP
.BI \-\-token " T"
The registration token, for a switchboard that requires one.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " TEXT"
Announce this name to the nodes you call, through a switchboard that carries names.
It is a claim, never proof.
.TP
.B \-\-listed
List the name and number in the switchboard's directory.
.TP
.BR \-\-answer " " ask | known | auto | never
The answer policy.
The default is
.BR ask .
.TP
.BI \-\-splice " NIC"
Attach to a LAN: frames go straight on that NIC.
Needs
.B CAP_NET_RAW
once.
.TP
.BI \-\-site\-key " FILE"
The secret shared by every splice box on one LAN segment, 64 hex digits.
.TP
.BI \-\-jack " TAP"
Attach a tap device you own.
.TP
.BI \-\-sock\-port " LOCAL:PEER"
Frames as datagrams on a Unix socket: for tests.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " [ADDR:]PORT"
The UDP address to listen on.
A bare port is IPv4 and IPv6 on any address;
.BI [ ADDR ] :PORT
is IPv6; a comma separates several.
.TP
.BI \-\-peer " BUNDLE[@HOST:PORT]"
A peer known by its bundle file.
Used without a switchboard.
.TP
.BI \-\-authority " KEY"
Found a line run by the operator whose public key this is.
.TP
.BI \-\-admit " ADMIT"
The operator's admit for this node (hex, or a file).
.TP
.BR \-\-relay " " yes | no | auto
Relay for members of your line;
.B auto
only when this node has a public address.
.TP
.BR \-\-nat\-pmp " " on | off
Ask the home router to keep a port open (default on).
.TP
.BI \-\-direct\-retry " S"
How often a relayed peer tries for a direct path.
.TP
.BI \-\-underlay " DEV"
The network device to reach peers by when the jack has an address in their network (default: the default route's).
The node sends such traffic through a socket tied to that device, so it does not go into the jack.
.TP
.B \-\-everything
Send all of this machine's traffic through the jack's own routing table (made by
.BR "longwire jack \-\-everything" ).
Every socket of the engine is tied to the real network device (the default route's, or
.BR \-\-underlay ),
so the line's own packets never go into the line; when that device changes, the node lets go of the jack and
.B up
starts it again on the new one.
Needs
.BR \-\-jack .
.B up
adds it by itself when the jack's table already takes everything.
.TP
.B \-\-use\-exit
Hand every frame for the exit gateway (169.254.77.1, whose MAC 02:6c:77:65:78:74 the jack helper fixes beside it when
.B longwire jack \-\-everything
has no
.BR \-\-via )
to the member that shares its internet, with that member's own MAC put in, and to nobody else.
With no such member the frames are dropped and counted, and nothing goes out.
.B up
adds it from a profile with
.B everything = yes
and no
.BR gateway ,
and by itself when the jack's table goes to that gateway.
.TP
.BI \-\-exit\-through " NUMBER"
The member to go out through when more than one shares; it must offer, or nothing goes out.
Without it: the only one; with several sharing, none is used.
Implies
.BR \-\-use\-exit .
.TP
.B \-\-exit
Share this machine's internet: tell every member of the line, inside its session, every 10 seconds, the MAC of the jack.
.B longwire jack \-\-share
does the kernel's side.
Not with
.BR \-\-use\-exit ;
needs
.BR \-\-jack ,
or
.BR \-\-exit\-mac .
.TP
.BI \-\-exit\-mac " MAC"
On a spliced LAN: the way out is the machine with this MAC (its router), not a jack.
Implies
.BR \-\-exit .
.TP
.BI \-\-keepalive " S" "\fR, \fP" \-\-rekey " S"
Keepalive (25) and rekey (120) intervals, in seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-sb\-rekey " S"
The rekey interval of a switchboard session (600).
.TP
.B \-\-cand\-loopback
Offer 127.x as a call candidate: tests, and two nodes on one host.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " FILE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-sock " PATH" "\fR, \fP" \-\-state " DIR"
Where the identity, the control socket and the pins live.
.TP
.BI \-\-group " NAME|GID"
Give the control socket to that group at mode 0660 (the services use the group
.BR longwire ).
.TP
.BI \-\-maxpeers " N"
How many peers to hold (default 32).
.SH "ANSWER POLICY"
.TP
.B ask
Every ring waits for
.B longwire pickup
or
.BR "longwire reject" .
This is the default.
.TP
.B known
Numbers you have been connected to before are answered; the rest wait for you.
.TP
.B auto
Anyone is answered.
For headless machines you have decided to trust.
.TP
.B never
Every ring is refused.
.PP
A node vouched for by a member of your line, or holding the operator's admit,
is answered under every policy except
.BR never .
.PP
Both ends show the same eight-word
.IR "safety phrase" ,
a moment after the call connects (the ring shows none):
it is committed to before either end could pick it, and tied to the session.
Compare it on another channel, and hang up at once if the words differ.
Every session reached through a switchboard has one, a fellow member's and a relayed one included; the end with the lower key id commits, whoever dialled.
A call whose other end keeps talking and does not take part in the exchange within 10 seconds is ended; see
.BR longwire-security (7).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.longwire/seed
Your identity: 32 bytes, mode 0600.
It is your number; back it up
.RB ( "longwire key export" ).
.TP
.I ~/.longwire/ctl
The control socket of the running node.
.TP
.I ~/.longwire/known
The numbers whose keys you have pinned.
.TP
.I ~/.longwire/answer
The current answer policy.
.TP
.I ~/.longwire/log
What the background node (\fBlongwire on\fP) said.
.TP
.I ~/.config/longwire/profiles
Saved connections.
Mode 0600, because it holds tokens.
See
.BR longwire-profiles (5).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/longwire/jack
The root helper that makes the tap device, with its address and routes, run through polkit (org.longwire.jack).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/longwire/jack\-everything
The same helper as a second polkit action (org.longwire.everything): it may also send everything through the line, or share this machine's internet.
.TP
.I /run/longwire/share\-lwN
What the jack helper changed to share this machine's internet through the jack
.BR lwN ,
so that it is undone exactly.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/longwire/site
The root helper that makes this machine a site node (writes
.I /etc/longwire/NIC.conf
and starts
.BR longwire@NIC ),
run through polkit.
.TP
.I /etc/longwire/NIC.conf
Arguments of the service
.BR longwire@NIC.service .
.TP
.I /run/longwire/NIC/ctl
The control socket of the service
.BR longwire@NIC ,
mode 0660, group
.BR longwire .
.TP
.I /var/lib/longwire/NIC/
The service's state; its identity is
.I .longwire/seed
inside.
It is a link to
.IR /var/lib/private/longwire/NIC ,
which only root can enter.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LW_DIR
The directory of the identity and state (default
.IR ~/.longwire ).
.TP
.B LW_SEED
The identity file (default
.IR $LW_DIR/seed ).
.TP
.B LW_SOCK
The control socket (default
.IR $LW_DIR/ctl ).
To use a service node:
.BR LW_SOCK=/run/longwire/eth0/ctl .
.TP
.B LW_CONF
The directory of the profiles file (default
.IR ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/longwire ).
.TP
.B LONGWIRE_MOD
The module to load, when it is not on hibr's module path.
.TP
.B LONGWIRE_HIBR
The hibr executable that
.B on
starts (default
.BR hibr ).
.TP
.B LONGWIRE_JACK
The jack helper to run.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, no colour, even on a terminal.
.TP
.B LONGWIRE_COLOR
.B always
or
.B never
colours the sign that starts each sentence, or does not, whatever else says; otherwise only on a terminal.
.TP
.B LONGWIRE_SITE
The site helper to run.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed; the reason is on standard error.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Save a connection and turn it on in the background:
.PP
.nf
.RS
longwire profile set home switchboard=203.0.113.7:6741/lw1q7m...k2a \e
        token=5fe1c0ab attach=jack interface=lw0
longwire on
.RE
.fi
.PP
Call another node, and answer on the other end:
.PP
.nf
.RS
longwire dial 312-884-076
longwire pickup
.RE
.fi
.PP
Share this machine's internet with the line, on the machine that has it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
longwire profile set home address=192.168.152.1/24
longwire exit on
.RE
.fi
.PP
and send everything out through it, on another member:
.PP
.nf
.RS
longwire profile set home address=192.168.152.2/24 everything=yes dns=1.1.1.1
.RE
.fi
.PP
Watch the node narrate itself:
.PP
.nf
.RS
longwire watch
.RE
.fi
.SH SECURITY
The handshake combines X25519 with ML-KEM-768, so a quantum computer would have to break ML-KEM-768 as well as X25519 to read recorded traffic;
the data is ChaCha20-Poly1305 with keys renewed every two minutes.
The composition has not been formally analysed or independently reviewed, and the Ed25519 signatures that vouch for line membership are not post-quantum.
See
.BR longwire-security (7).
